<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  A second volume, so tiering can be seen.
  
  `ALTER TABLE … MOVE PARTITION TO VOLUME` was left out of Flint on the grounds
  that this server had one disk, and a control for moving data between volumes
  that has never moved any is not a control worth shipping. This is the fixture
  that removes the excuse: a `cold` disk and a `tiered` policy with two volumes,
  so a partition can actually be moved and watched arrive.
  
  Note what a stock server looks like without it. `system.disks` can hold disks
  that belong to no policy — the backup disk does — and a disk in no policy can
  never hold a part, which is a fact worth reading off the page rather than
  discovering by trying to move something there.
  
  Applied from `config.d` on boot. To add it to a running container:
  
      docker cp contrib/dev-storage.xml \
        flint-dev-clickhouse-1:/etc/clickhouse-server/config.d/flint-storage.xml
  
  A new disk needs a restart: unlike users, storage configuration is not
  hot-reloaded in full.
-->
<clickhouse>
  <storage_configuration>
    <disks>
      <cold>
        <path>/var/lib/clickhouse/cold/</path>
      </cold>
    </disks>
    <policies>
      <tiered>
        <volumes>
          <hot>
            <disk>default</disk>
            <!-- Small on purpose: a part over this size is written straight to
                 the next volume, which is the behaviour worth being able to
                 demonstrate on a development machine. -->
            <max_data_part_size_bytes>1048576</max_data_part_size_bytes>
          </hot>
          <cold>
            <disk>cold</disk>
          </cold>
        </volumes>
        <!-- Move data down when the hot volume is over 80% full. -->
        <move_factor>0.2</move_factor>
      </tiered>
    </policies>
  </storage_configuration>
</clickhouse>
<!--
  A table on the tiered policy, to move partitions of:

      CREATE TABLE analytics.tiered_probe (d Date, v UInt32)
        ENGINE = MergeTree PARTITION BY toYYYYMM(d) ORDER BY d
        SETTINGS storage_policy = 'tiered';
      INSERT INTO analytics.tiered_probe SELECT toDate('2026-01-15'), number FROM numbers(200);
      INSERT INTO analytics.tiered_probe SELECT toDate('2026-02-15'), number FROM numbers(200);

  What that fixture demonstrates, twice measured: the hot volume's free ratio on
  a development machine is 0.197 against a move factor of 0.2, so the server
  drains it continuously. A partition moved onto it — by hand or through Flint —
  is back on `cold` within ten seconds. The page says so before offering the
  control, which is the whole reason the control was worth building here.
-->
